{"name":"Woven Wicker","hex":"#b99974","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"woven","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Fabricated by weaving.","Interlaced"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A cloth formed by weaving. It only stretches in the bias directions (between the warp and weft directions), unless the threads are elastic."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/woven"},{"term":"wicker","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A flexible branch or twig of a plant such as willow, used in weaving baskets and furniture.","Wickerwork."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of wickerwork."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wicker"}],"entries":[{"title":"Woven fabric","blocks":[{"text":"Woven fabric is any textile formed by weaving. Woven fabrics, often created on a loom, are made of many threads woven in a warp and weft. Technically, a woven fabric is any fabric made by interlacing two or more threads at right angles to one another."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Palaung_Woman_Kalaw_Shan_Myanmar.jpg/330px-Palaung_Woman_Kalaw_Shan_Myanmar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woven_fabric"},{"title":"Wicker","blocks":[{"text":"Wicker is a method of weaving used to make products such as furniture and baskets, as well as a descriptor to classify such products. It is the oldest furniture-making method, dating as far back as c. 3000 BC. Wicker was first documented in ancient Egypt, then having been made from pliable plant material, but in modern times it is made from any pliable, easily woven material. The word wicker or \"wisker\" is believed to be of Scandinavian origin: vika, which means \"to fold\" in Swedish. Wicker is traditionally made of plant-based material, such as willow, rattan, reed, and bamboo, though the term also applies to products woven from synthetic fibers. Wicker is light yet sturdy, making it suitable for items that will be moved often like porch and patio furniture. Rushwork and wickerwork are terms used in England. A typical braiding pattern is called Wiener Geflecht, Viennese braiding, as it was invented in 18th century Vienna and later most prominently used with the Thonet coffeehouse chair."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Apples_in_basket_2018_G1.jpg/330px-Apples_in_basket_2018_G1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wicker"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Fabric_yarn_fiber.PNG/500px-Fabric_yarn_fiber.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Fabric_yarn_fiber.PNG/500px-Fabric_yarn_fiber.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An Arabic-language graphic distinguishing raw fibers from processed threads and woven fabric.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFabric_yarn_fiber.PNG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Leinengewebe.JPG/500px-Leinengewebe.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Leinengewebe.JPG/1280px-Leinengewebe.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Leinengewebe.JPG/500px-Leinengewebe.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Plain weave","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALeinengewebe.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Audiniu_pavyzdziai.jpg/500px-Audiniu_pavyzdziai.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d